1 NAME Peter (Petrus) /Poolye / Pawley / Pooley/
2 GIVN Peter (Petrus)
2 SURN Poolye / Pawley / Pooley
2 _MARNM Pooley
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1600
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Petrus Poolye mentioned in the record of Petrus Poolye and Elisabetha Johnn
2 CONT
2 CONT Name	Petrus Poolye
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse's Name	Elisabetha Johnn
2 CONT
2 CONT Event Date	06 Apr 1619
2 CONT
2 CONT Event Place	Blisland,Cornwall,England
2 CONT
2 CONT England Marriages, 1538–1973
2 CONT
2 CONT Indexing Project (Batch) Number	M00796-2
2 CONT
2 CONT System Origin	England-ODM
2 CONT
2 CONT GS Film number	546355, 564355
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Pooley Name Meaning habitational name from a place so called in Warwickshire. No forms of the name are recorded before the 13th century, when Povele, Poueleye, Powelee, Pouelee, and Poleye are all found. The second element is Old 
2 CONC English leah ‘wood’, ‘clearing’; the first is pofel, a word found occasionally in place names (but not attested independently), the meaning of which has not been established. habitational name from Pooley Bridge in Cumbria, so nam
2 CONC ed from Old English pol ‘pool’ + Old Norse haugr ‘hill’, ‘mound’. topographic name from Middle English pole ‘pool’ + ey ‘low-lying land’ or hey ‘enclosure’, or a habitational name from minor places originally named with these elem
2 CONC ents, such as Polly Shaw in Kent or the former Polleheye (13th-century), later Pooley (now named Hunt’s Hall) in Pebmarsh, Essex.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Dictionary of American Family Names ©2013, Oxford University Press
