1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William White, yeoman of Trewellard, made a will on Feb 7, 1831. The will ws proved June 17, 1831 and named his wife Mary (exec.) and five children. Witnesses were John Williams and Jane Williams.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is the will:
2 CONT
2 CONT The last Will and Testament of William White Senr. of Trewellard in the Parish of Saint Just
2 CONT
2 CONT in the County of Cornwall.
2 CONT
2 CONT First I give and bequeath to Mary my beloved Wife whome I likewise constitute make and ordain the sole Executrix of this my last Will and Testament all and singular My Effects, Goods, and Chattles, of whatsoever name or denominati
2 CONC on they may be called, by Her freely to be possessed and enjoyed, only to pay to each of my five Children the sum of one shilling, respectively, three months next after my decease, satisfying and confirming this and no other to be
2 CONC  my last Will and Testament. In Witness whereof I have hereunto set my Hand and Seal this Seventh day of February One Thousand Eight Hundred and Thirty one.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed sealed published pronounced
2 CONT
2 CONT and declared by the said William White
2 CONT
2 CONT as his last Will and Testament
2 CONT
2 CONT in presence of
2 CONT
2 CONT John Williams Wm.White
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Williams
