1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Note:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1841 Census at Nancherrow Row, St. Just in Penwith:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Trembath,40,,Miner,In county
2 CONT
2 CONT ,,Jane Trembath,,42,,In county
2 CONT
2 CONT ,,Hugh Trembath,17,,Miner,In county
2 CONT
2 CONT ,,John Trembath,15,,Tin Dresser,In county
2 CONT
2 CONT ,,Mary Trembath,,13,,In county
2 CONT
2 CONT ,,William Trembath,8,,,In county
2 CONT
2 CONT ,,Elizabeth Trembath,,6,,In county
2 CONT
2 CONT ,,Thomas Trembath,3,,,In county
2 CONT
2 CONT [Trem112598.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT !According to Barbara Lein, there were other children born to Hugh an d Amelia Trembath, but they died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Hugh Trembath became a citizen of the United States on March 9, 1895 . Bobbalee Ray Hughes has a photocopy of his certificate of citizens hip.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography: In the Daily Mining Journal, Marquette, Michigan, Nov. 30 , 1900:
2 CONT
2 CONT Celebrated Golden Wedding
2 CONT
2 CONT Oldest Married Couple in Republic had that Happiness
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Hugh Trembath of Republic, who have enjoyed the happin ess and endured the hardships of wedded life for fifty years, celebra ted their golden wedding on Friday evening of last week.
2 CONT
2 CONT The affair was quite unique and was carried out to the letter accor ding to the ideas of the worthy old couple. Something like two hundr ed innvitations were issued to friends in town to be present at the t own hall at a certain t
2 CONC ime that evening. It was whispered around tha t it was to be quite different from most golden weddings and few wh o had invitations remained away.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the apponted time Mr. and Mrs. Trembath advanced to the front o f the hall, accompanined by two little nephews, the sons of Mssers Ea de, and Tucker. Following them, were two nieces, little daughters o f the same gentlemen, and
2 CONC  W. J. Whinnen's little girl. One of the gi rls carried the wedding ring, which was displayed on a white satin pi llow. The stage was appropriately decorated, with gold figures "1850 -1900", prominently to the front. Rev. Harper p
2 CONC ronounced the marriag e vows while the aged couple stood under a bell, listening as attenti vely as they did when they were made man and wife fifty years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the ceremony, Mr. and Mrs. Trembath received the congratulati ons of all present. It was indeed a happy evening for them and the y were greatly pleased to meet wih many friends. Nearly all who turn ed out went provided with 
2 CONC gifts for the old couple and when all thes e were grouped in collections were very atractive.
2 CONT
2 CONT A bounteous feast was served and it was much enjoyed. Ater that th e older people retired to their homes while the younger folk remaine d unil early in the morning, enjoying several hours of dancing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Trembath are said to be the oldest married couple in R epublic. They are both blessed with excellent health and it is the w ish of their friends here that they live to enjoy many more years o f wedded life.
