1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Note:
2 CONT
2 CONT [Trem112598.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT !On October 8, 1908, Florence wrote the following letter from Republi c, Michigan to her cousin, Charlotte Trembath:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Cousin:
2 CONT
2 CONT I know you would be patiently waiting for a letter relating to ou r dear grandma's death so I wrote while I felt able. We are all sic k and broken hearted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Gr'ma would be brought up to our place before she died. We br ought her up last Friday afternoon. She was not up twentyfour hour s before she died.
2 CONT
2 CONT No one knows what she suffered. She was so patient during all he r illness. Her legs were swollen like stove pipes when she died. Sh e had dropsy so badly and kidney trouble.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gr'ma died at 9:20 Sat. morning. She was sensible to the last minu te and moved around a great deal. Her last words were "Give me a mou thful of cold water" She died sitting up. She was buried Tuesday af ternoon. Gr'ma's grave is 
2 CONC next to Aunt's two children. She always w anted to be buried by Jimmy and Walter and she had her wish.
2 CONT
2 CONT We have purchased the lot. Someday we are going to put a stone rai ling around the lot and we hope Uncle Jim will help us.
2 CONT
2 CONT We did everything Gr'ma wished but one thing. She never wanted t o be embalmed but had to have it done in order to keep her. The Doct or was afraid she would burst. Her stomach was like a barrel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her casket was lovely. It was oak inside and covered with broadclo th. There were five inches of padding in it and a beautiful satin pi llow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gr'ma was so afraid her casket would look bare. But she was certain ly buried well.
2 CONT
2 CONT Uncle Hugh sent $100 three weeks ago to pay all expenses. Mother t old him how sick Gr'ma was and he sent money to be prepared for it . It took $75 of the money to pay for the casket.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gr'ma's flowers were simply grand and she had stacks of them. Ever yone knew and respected her. People from different places sent flowe rs and some attended the funeral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gr'pa's and Gr'ma's pictures are in one of the Detroit newspapers w ith an account of their life and the length of time they have lived t ogether. So you may think how much they were thought of.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gr'pa talks of her all day long. He says "Poor mother. Mother, mo ther, mother. She's gone, gone forever and won't speak to me again . She will never have a cup of tea with me again." He says he know s he makes us feel worse but h
2 CONC e can't help it.
2 CONT
2 CONT I never thought he would break down so completely. He has cried hi mself sick. Now he is praying for the Lord to take him right away . Do you wonder how we can stand it? Mother is almost frantic. W e cannot spare him too just yet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aunt Ellen did not come nor my sister Olive either. Uncle Hugh wa s too far away. Mother was alone. How she wished some one had come.
2 CONT
2 CONT We had to pass Gr'ma's house to go to town. We always went in fo r a few minutes at least. I never missed many days in not going ther e. Now we have no place to go in the evenings. I really wish the ho use would burn down for then
2 CONC  it will not be so hard for us to pass th ere.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother is going to send you each something of Gr'ma's. Aunt Susa n is to have her best black dress. If it does not suit her, it can b e fixed for you. It is a lovely dress.
2 CONT
2 CONT You will find enclosed a flower out of the wreath we grandchildre n gave. We had "Grandma" worked in it. My! but it was pretty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gr'ma was buried in a black satin skirt and a black taffeta dressin g sacque with white ruche (?) around the throat. She looked so peace ful.
2 CONT
2 CONT Everyone said she looked as though she was so happy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gr'ma was always afraid she would not have an easy death. Many i s the times she prayed to the Lord to grant her an easy death and h e did.
2 CONT
2 CONT I watched her die. Not one muscle moved. She was sitting up whe n she died and one of the neighbors was fanning her. In fact she ha d to be fanned every minute for three days because her breath was s o short.
2 CONT
2 CONT The choir sang "Nearer My God To Thee", "Rock Of Ages" and "Waiting ". The sermon began like this: In my father's mansions are many sou ls." I don't remember just where it is. This is not correct but al l I remember.
2 CONT
2 CONT Well Lottie dear you must excuse all mistakes and inconsistencies f or I am writing under difficulties.
2 CONT
2 CONT I could write you much more but I'm too grief stricken just at pres ent. This will satisfy you for the time. I sincerely hope you wil l answer by return mail for Gr'pa is waiting to hear from you.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the way your mother knows Mr. Cowlin. He was one of the pall be arers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother wants Richie and his wife to write to her and also Maud. Sh e is so lonesome already. I hope they won't hesitate in doing so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Well good bye sweetheart for the time with love and kisses to all f rom your heart broken cousin. Florence.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Hugh Trembath's notes for information on their golden wedding ann iversary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography: Contributed by Barbara Tucker
2 CONT
2 CONT RELI Methodist
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh and Amelia were said to have had 13 children in all. Many die d before they left Cornwall. Listed in the I.G. I. as christened i n St. Just-in-Penwith were: Elizabeth christened 11 February 1855, A melia Jane christened 1 Feb
2 CONC  1857, Hugh christened 1 February 1857, Am elia Jane christened 21 December 1863. Evidently, they all died as t he same names were used again for future children.
2 CONT
2 CONT No record was found for Richard's christening but he showed up in th e 1870 census in Hancock, MI.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amelia had a granddaughter (born to Elizabeth Ellen Trembath Tucker 1 0 Jul 1886) who was named Elizabeth Hattie Walls Tucker. Elizabeth H attie Walls Tucker later married William Trebilcock and they lived i n Michigan.
