1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The West Australian (Perth) Wednesday 8 December 1937
2 CONT
2 CONT FUNERAL
2 CONT
2 CONT The Late Mrs. G. L. Holder
2 CONT
2 CONT The beautiful chapel of the Karrakatta Crematorium was full on Saturday afternoon when friends gathered for the impressive cremation service of the late Mrs. Grace Leggo Holder, who passed away at her home, 109 St. Leonard’s-avenu
2 CONC e, West Leederville, on Thursday last. The Rev. D. J. Davies, of St. Barnabas’s Church, West Leederville, met the cortege at the chapel steps and, to the accompaniment of the organ playing “The Rosary,” the procession proceeded up
2 CONC  the aisle and the casket was placed on the catafalque around which the many and beautiful floral tributes were placed. After the service and the touching remarks of Mr. Davies, to whom the deceased had been known for some time, t
2 CONC he electrically manipulated organ again peeled forth, this time accompanying a soloist who sang the familiar hymn, “Nearer, My God, to Thee, thus creating an atmosphere of deep reverence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. G. L. Holder, who was born in Pendeen, St. Just, Cornwall, in 1862, was a daughter of the late Elizabeth and Richard Bennetts, the latter a Cornish mining engineer who arrived in Victoria from California (U.S.A.) in the early
2 CONC  days of goldmining, and later returned to his native country. In 1874 Mrs. Holder married the late Mr. Harry Holder, a native of Cheltenham, Gloucestershire, who predeceased her in March, 1936. Accompanied by their nine children 
2 CONC they arrived in Western Australia in 1912 with hopes of meeting fair fortune, but the long war period took its toll. As each son became eligible he enlisted for active service. Their son Jim embarked with the 11th Battalion on the
2 CONC  first convoy to leave Australia, while the youngest son and the fifth to enlist embarked on the last troopship. The sixth son remained on home service. Suspense, deaths and prolonged illness of the returned eventually caused Mrs.
2 CONC  Holder to have a stroke in 1921, through which she became blind. In spite of her affliction, however, she was an outstanding personality in both physique and cheerfulness. She was a keen politician and patriot and took an active 
2 CONC interest in world affairs up to a few days before her death. A daughter, Martha, predeceased her a few years ago. Two sons and two daughters survive her, also three sisters, two of whom are in the United States and one in Cornwall
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The chief mourners were Grace and Mary (daughters), Walter and Will (sons), Madame Laurette E. D. Holder (daughter-in-law), Enid Grace, Harry and Francis Holder and John and Ivan Griffiths, Mrs. I. Sykes (niece), Miss Gladys Cust,
2 CONC  Mrs. J. Zelling and Mrs. W. Howard. The pall-bearers were Messrs. J. Booty, F. Gratwick, W. Henville, J. Patterson, P. Wishart and M. Collins.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cornishman (Penzance) Thursday 6 January 1938
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATHS
2 CONT
2 CONT HOLDER.—On December 2nd, 1937, at 109, St. Leonard’s Ave., Perth, Australia, Grace Leggo, widow of the late Harry Holder, and loving sister of M. A. Bennetts, 8, Weethes Cottages, Penzance, aged 75.
2 CONT
2 CONT Transcriber’s note: Born St Just in Penwith, daughter of Richard BENNETTS and Elizabeth Woolcock LEGGO.
