1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Edward signed the protestation roll in 1641...... He paid 3 Hearths on the Hearth Tax Roll in 1660
2 CONT
2 CONT EDWARD CHIRGWIN   (1611-1687)
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Chirgwin was born in Sancreed Parish, Cornwall in 1611.  On 28 April 1635, when aged 24 he married 20 year old Cheston Edwards at Sancreed.  Edward was a yeoman, a small landowner engaged in agriculture classified as middle
2 CONC  class.  He had full ownership in two properties and part ownership of three others, all probably quite small.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward and Cheston were married six years before their first child was born and then had 7 children over the next 16 years.  The last child was born when Cheston was aged 42.  The children were Margaret (born 1641 and married Thom
2 CONC as Taylor when she was 22), Edward (born 1643 who remained a bachelor until he was 40 when he married Blanch Grosse), Cheston ( born 1647 and married John Richards), Elizabeth (born 1650 and probably died young), Abraham (born 165
2 CONC 1 and married Elizabeth Baynard when she was 30), William (born 1655 and died a few days later) and James (born 1657 and was 27 when he married Jean Edwards).
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward’s will was written in 1686,  few months before his death in February 1687 at the age of 76.  This will and the inventory of his assets tell us quite a bit about his lifestyle.  Edward commenced his will in the traditional m
2 CONC anner for the day by commending his soul to “almighty God my creator and redeemer” and with a small bequest to the minister of 2 shillings and 6 pence for “tithes forgotten”.  Also, 10 shillings was left to the poor of his Parish 
2 CONC Sancreed, and a similar amount to the poor at St Just and Madron suggesting regular contact with these adjoining Parishes.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Godsons and Goddaughters received one shilling each and his grandchildren 10 shillings each except the children of John Richards who were to receive only 6 pence each.  His one great grandchild received 5 shillings.  The two c
2 CONC hildren of his brother Thomas were left £1 each.  Edward’s daughter Margaret had just been widowed and she was left £20, but the other daughter Cheston, wife of John Richards only received one shilling.  The land and buildings wer
2 CONC e divided between Edward’s wife, Cheston, eldest son Edward and youngest son James.  Second son Abraham was left all goods and chattels jointly with his mother Cheston and these two were appointed joint executors.  The paultry amo
2 CONC unts left to Cheston Richards and her children may have been because her husband John Richards were financially secure.  The will was signed “E. Chirgwin” and considering the illiteracy at that time it appears that Edward Chirgwin
2 CONC  was above average in education as well as in wealth.
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventory of goods and chattels totalled £359  5 shillings with the major items being the 5 properties with a total value of £200.  The next highest valued asset was the livestock consisting of 20 cows, 26 steers and heifers, 
2 CONC 5 labouring horses, 4 oxen, an ass, 22 sheep and lambs, 13 pigs and a colt.  Total value £86.  Assets also included an outstanding debt of £32 and some silver spoons and tin valued at £14.  Farm implements, household goods and fur
2 CONC niture had very little value.  Three beds, two tables, sideboards, trunks and a cupboard did not total £10.  In fact the highest valued item in the house were his clothes.  “weringe aparrill ...  boots, shoos and hatt”  £4  6 shil
2 CONC lings and 8 pence.
