0 @N6000000004672193342@ NOTE Mayor of Penzance's Diary 1816/17.
1 CONT
1 CONT 13 June 1817.   Heard a complaint of Ann Hosking of Trereiffe against Henry Trewhella of Madron for stealing 2 sacks of wheat from the market yesterday. Prisoner denied any knowledge but was committed for trial.
1 CONT
1 CONT 18 July 1817: Trinity Sessions; William Trewhella indicted for stealing sacks with corn in the market, when he pleaded guilty. To be imprisoned another week, then publicly whipped.
1 CONT 24 July 1817 William Trewhella publicly whipped according to his sentence round the market and discharged.
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------- ---------------------------
1 CONT The Green Market was also used as a cattle market and this with the accommodation of booths and stalls for the sale of all sorts of commodities must have made progress difficult on market days and fair times.
1 CONT
1 CONT 18th July. Henry Trewhella for stealing two sacks of wheat in the market was sentenced to a week’s imprisonment and to be whipped through the market on the next Thursday. On 21st July the prison was broken open and he might have e
1 CONC scaped, but he refused to do so, saying he would take his punishment, and he was publicly whipped in accordance with his sentence.
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------- ----------------------------
1 CONT Internet Forum:- Re: Mayor of Penzance's diary 1816/17
1 CONT « Reply #2 on Nov 4, 2007, 10:02am »
1 CONT
1 CONT Just had another look at this latest and have discovered a little problem that I need to look into.
1 CONT
1 CONT All events took place in 1817 which is wherein the problem lies.
1 CONT
1 CONT I had it in mind that the Henry Trewhella was the man who later married Elizabeth Honeychurch at Madron in 1834 (and it could still have been him).
1 CONT
1 CONT But this Henry would have been only 12 at the time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Very possible but the fact he was 'committed for trial' is possibly an indication that the offender 'may' have been older.
1 CONT
1 CONT If so then it would have been his father who was the offender.
1 CONT
1 CONT The next problem is the William Trewhella of the same year who served his sentence of a public whipping.
1 CONT
1 CONT If he is who I thought he was then he would have been only 10 years old at the time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Given the times of these offences, and the laws and punishments of the era, it is still quite likely that Henry and William were indeed the two I had identified.
1 CONT
1 CONT If so, then it was their nephew who was sent to Tasmania.
1 CONT
1 CONT Must look into this some more when I get the time to sit and relax and look properly at all the information.
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------- ------------------
1 CONT NB.  The two offences above, committed by Henry and William, appear to be one and the same offence committed by Henry, the second offence where the name was given as William was actually repeating the first report.  Henry was comm
1 CONC itted for trial on 13th June and tried on 18th July.
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------- --------------------
1 CONT FILE - Quarter Sessions Order Book - ref.  QS/1/11  - date: April 1825-Jan 1831
1 CONT item: Sessions held at Truro - ref.  QS/1/11/1-30  - date: 12 April 1825
1 CONT [from Scope and Content] Recognizance of John Williams jun. of Madron, yeo., to answer in bastardy, and Henry Trewhella, his pledge: estreated.
1 CONT item: Sessions held at Lostwithiel - ref.  QS/1/11/31-65  - date: 12 July 1825
1 CONT [from Scope and Content] Henry Trewhella of Madron, yeoman surety of above J. W. jun: recognizance discharged.
0 @N6000000004672193357@ NOTE Loveday Rowe, aged 75 years, Independent, b. Cornwall, was with the family at Census in 1841.
0 @N6000000004672193414@ NOTE FILE - Quarter Sessions Order Book - ref.  QS/1/12  - date: Apr 1831-Mar 1836
1 CONT item: Sessions held at Bodmin - ref.  QS/1/12/1-27  - date: 15 April 1831
1 CONT [from Scope and Content] James Trewhella of Madron, lab., indicted for stealing one bundle of barley straw and another bundle of straw, from Elizabeth Smith: one month's hard labour in Bodmin gaol.
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------- -------------------------------------------------------------------------- ------------------------------------------
1 CONT A still-born son was born to a Mrs James Trewhella at Chyandour on 30th Nov, 1850 (reported in the "West Briton").
1 CONT The West Briton, Cornwall, Thursday, January 13th, 1887:- "Death. TREWHELLA - Chyandour, Penzance, January 11th.  Mr. James G. Trewhella, aged 78."
0 @N6000000004672193734@ NOTE Also living or staying at the family home in Hea, Madron on Census night 1881 was Martin T.
1 CONT Nankervis, a nephew, aged 20 years, unmarried, born Madron. He was a Prudential Insurance
1 CONT Agent. He was also with them at Census in 1871 aged 11 years.
